62304688, -0.0188140869140625, 0.0175628662109375, 0.006076812744140625, -0.007110595703125, 0.038848876953125, -0.02349853515625, 0.01355743408203125, 0.009002685546875, 0.01158905029296875, 0.01410675048828125, 0.00235748291015625, 0.0400390625, -0.01061248779296875, 0.045013427734375, 0.0037097930908203125, 0.03594970703125, -0.007534027099609375, 0.001964569091796875, 0.003269195556640625, -0.01097869873046875, 0.002166748046875, -0.0028133392333984375, -0.0170745849609375, 0.0194091796875, 0.046112060546875, -0.02679443359375, -0.0178985595703125, -0.0313720703125, 0.02960205078125, -0.01108551025390625, -0.0030078887939453125, 0.047119140625, 0.0238189697265625, -0.00658416748046875, -0.041015625, -0.0135345458984375, -0.0007715225219726562, -0.0023212432861328125, -0.03399658203125, 0.01556396484375, -0.0306243896484375, -0.01300048828125, -0.0009512901306152344, -0.007659912109375, 0.022674560546875, 0.01751708984375, -0.0059661865234375, -0.01389312744140625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines often optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, missing opportunities to leverage shared structure across tasks. A key challenge is not only curating each dataset, but also organizing diverse supervision according to the dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\nWe introduce a capability-driven data infrastructure\u2014a framework that builds and schedules data according to the progression of model capabilities. Here, a \"capability\" refers to a distinct generative skill, such as text-to-image synthesis, image editing, or associating images with external knowledge. Our approach combines two main components: (1) capability-specific supervision construction, and (2) curriculum scheduling aligned with the order in which capabilities are acquired.\n\nThe infrastructure consists of three specialized, interoperable data engines:\n1. The grounding engine constructs text-image pairs for text-to-image (T2I) generation, focusing on diverse and accurate captions.\n2. The transformation engine creates inter-image editing pairs, supporting tasks like object insertion, removal, or style transfer.\n3. The association engine links images to structured knowledge entities, enabling image-knowledge association.\n\nThese engines are coordinated by caption experts, which align supervision across tasks and granularities, ensuring consistency and transferability. For example, captions are harmonized between T2I and editing data to facilitate cross-task learning.\n\nA multi-stage curriculum schedules data exposure based on capability dependencies. Early stages emphasize foundational T2I grounding, followed by editing and knowledge association as the model matures. The curriculum evolves task composition, visual concept distribution, data quality, and image resolution in a staged manner.\n\nCapability-aware evaluation closes the loop by identifying gaps and guiding data resampling. This process involves: (a) targeted retrieval to find underrepresented concepts or tasks, (b) expert construction to generate or refine supervision for these gaps, and (c) gap-aware resampling to rebalance the dataset accordingly.\n\nFor data curation and quality assurance, we apply automated filtering for image quality, deduplication, and content safety, combined with expert-guided recaptioning and entity linking. Only data meeting strict quality and diversity criteria are included.\n\nAt scale, our framework curates a 440M-image T2I corpus, 120M editing pairs, and over 27M image-entity pairs. We train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at two parameter scales: 3B and 6B. Training follows standard diffusion protocols, with curriculum-based data sampling and progressive image resolution. We compare our models against established baselines on CPI-Bench, a comprehensive benchmark for generative capabilities.\n\nEvaluation includes quantitative metrics such as FID (Fr\u00e9chet Inception Distance), CLIP score, and retrieval accuracy, as well as qualitative assessments by human raters for image fidelity, diversity, and editing precision. \"Broad visual coverage\" refers to the model's ability to generate images across a wide range of concepts and styles, while \"versatile rendering\" measures the model's adaptability to different tasks and prompts.\n\nExperimental results demonstrate that our infrastructure enables models to achieve strong performance across text-to-image, editing, and knowledge association tasks, with effective transfer and robust visual diversity."}}